# gitleaks allowlist — one fingerprint per line:  <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>
#
# Added 2026-08-09 after the first full-history scan of this repo
# (`gitleaks git`) reported "leaks found: 4".
#
# ⚠️ Why the pre-commit hook never caught these: the hook runs
# `gitleaks git --staged` and only sees the diff being committed. A secret in
# an old commit is invisible to it forever. A clean hook run is not evidence of
# a clean repo — only a full-history scan is.
#
# ── NONE OF THESE ARE FALSE POSITIVES ───────────────────────────────────────
# All four were real credentials. All four have already been remediated in the
# WORKING TREE — that is why the current files scan clean — but the values stay
# in git history permanently. These entries stop the findings blocking every
# push; they are not a statement that the credentials are safe.
#
# THE FIX IS ROTATION, NOT HISTORY REWRITING. Rotating renders the historical
# value worthless in one step, with none of the risk of rewriting history.
# Tracked in home-automation/TODO.md → "Rotate credentials left in git history".
#
# 1-2. Home Assistant long-lived access token, inline in the voice-bench
#      configs. Since refactored to `ha_token_file: "./secrets/ha_token"`.
#      ⚠️ HIGHEST PRACTICAL SEVERITY HERE: an HA LLAT grants full API control of
#      the house — locks, cameras, everything — and HA long-lived tokens do not
#      expire. Delete the old token in HA (profile → Long-lived access tokens)
#      and confirm secrets/ha_token holds a NEW one, not the same value.
